            <div class="SDC_EXPERT_INFO">
              <div class="SDC_INFO_NOTE"><p><strong>In short:</strong> SDC encrypts messages and files before they are sent. Discord profile names are display-only; cryptographic identity relies on stable identifiers and keys belonging to accounts and devices.</p></div>
              <h6>💬 Direct messages</h6>
              <p>Each device has its own independent secure session. Keys evolve automatically as the conversation continues instead of relying on one permanent key. This helps limit the impact if a temporary key is ever compromised.</p>
              <p>When several devices are authorized, SDC protects the message separately for each one. If an expected device cannot be covered securely, sending may be blocked instead of silently ignoring that device.</p>
              <h6>⚛️ Post-quantum protection</h6>
              <p>SDC combines conventional encryption with an additional protection designed to better withstand future quantum computers. Once this protection is active for a device, SDC avoids silently falling back to a less protected mode.</p>
              <p>If a device is temporarily unavailable, SDC can use an encrypted offline transport when authenticated and still-valid cryptographic information for that exact device is available.</p>
              <h6>🤝 Key exchange and identity</h6>
              <p>Key exchange lets both people establish a shared secret without sending that secret directly. SDC signs and verifies important steps so forged or replayed responses can be detected.</p>
              <p><strong>Discord usernames, display names, avatars and nicknames never participate in encryption.</strong> You can rename your profile without changing your keys or session. Identity relies on the stable Discord ID, the SDC identity and device identifiers.</p>
              <p>Identity verification is used to detect a real cryptographic key change. If the cryptographic identity truly changes, SDC can require a new verification instead of trusting it automatically.</p>
              <h6>📎 Files and attachments</h6>
              <p>Files are encrypted before upload and have their own protection, separate from message text. For newly encrypted files, the real filename is also kept inside the encrypted data so it can be restored even if Discord changes the outer filename.</p>
              <p>Discord can still learn service metadata required for operation, such as the fact that a file exists and its approximate size.</p>
              <h6>⌨️ Chat Control</h6>
              <p>Chat Control keeps the draft in an isolated input until encryption. Its goal is to reduce the number of places where plaintext exists inside Discord's interface before send.</p>
              <p>Disabling it does not disable encryption: while the SDC lock is enabled, Discord's normal composer can be used and the message is encrypted at send time.</p>
              <h6>🗄️ Local database</h6>
              <p>Keys and state required by SDC are stored in an encrypted local database protected by your password. Some installation-specific keys deliberately stay tied to that device rather than turning an export into a cloned device identity.</p>
              <h6>🕵️ What SDC does not hide</h6>
              <div class="SDC_INFO_WARN"><p><strong>Discord still sees:</strong> the accounts communicating, channel, timing, the existence of messages and files, approximate sizes, and some service-required metadata. The choice of a native sticker also remains visible to Discord.</p><p><strong>SDC protects content, not an already compromised computer:</strong> a keylogger, screen capture, malware, or sufficiently privileged Discord/Electron code can bypass protection implemented on that same computer.</p><p>SDC uses standard cryptographic mechanisms, but that should not be confused with a complete independent security audit of the plugin.</p></div>
              <details class="SDC_TECH_DETAILS"><summary>Technical details</summary><p>Direct messages: SDC4 Double Ratchet with encrypted headers, AES-256-GCM, HKDF-SHA-256 and P-521.</p><p>Post-quantum: ML-KEM-768 in hybrid mode, with SDC4Q/SDC4QF for compatible offline sends.</p><p>Files: DMs use per-file SDC4FSF1 forward-secret ciphertext; LIVE sessions carry the file CEK inside authenticated SDC4 application plaintext, while compatible offline DMs use the frozen SDC4Q/SDC4QF transport. GROUP-key channels with a distributed v1 file epoch use a fresh per-file CEK and SDC4FSF1 body; the CEK is wrapped by the generation file secret inside authenticated SDC3 v3 application plaintext. Legacy GROUP keys without that epoch, and other non-DM key types, retain the historical SDC3AEAD v3 file plane. The real filename is included inside authenticated encrypted content for newly encrypted files.</p><p>Local database: Argon2id for the password and AES-256-GCM for local envelopes.</p></details>
            </div>
